1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:01,560
Hello.

2
00:00:01,890 --> 00:00:06,150
So now we start the second section in our course.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:08,460
And this is grammar.

4
00:00:09,210 --> 00:00:14,280
In this section, you are going to have a lot of different interesting grammar topics.

5
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:20,850
So the first lesson in this grammar section is Talking about future.

6
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:30,810
We all need to talk about future and we know that in grammar there is a future tense.

7
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:32,790
Or there are future tenses.

8
00:00:32,790 --> 00:00:35,880
There is future simple, there is future continuous, future

9
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:36,600
perfect.

10
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:41,670
But do we really use those tenses a lot?

11
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:50,520
That is the question, yes, because most of the time or lots lots of times we use, we speak about

12
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:57,390
future, but we use other tenses and other grammatical structures to talk about future.

13
00:00:57,390 --> 00:01:07,410
And at this point we need to ask ourselves and look, look at what actually we want to talk about.

14
00:01:07,410 --> 00:01:15,840
And depending on what is the purpose of our sentence that determines what grammatical structure we need

15
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:20,640
to use and not always it's going to be future tense.

16
00:01:21,210 --> 00:01:25,470
So let's look at this list.

17
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:29,580
So talking about future, what can we be talking about?

18
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:34,650
What can we need to discuss in about the future?

19
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:35,640
In the future.

20
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:47,460
And here you see a list of different reasons for using some kind of a future grammar structure.

21
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:55,470
For example, lots of the times we speak about our plans, so plans and intentions, something that

22
00:01:55,470 --> 00:01:57,630
we plan or intend to do.

23
00:01:57,750 --> 00:01:59,460
This is future.

24
00:01:59,460 --> 00:01:59,870
Yeah.

25
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:07,230
So and we use and we talk about it a lot in our day to day life, in our work and so on.

26
00:02:07,230 --> 00:02:13,050
Yes, because we plan things, we express our intentions to do something.

27
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:22,800
Then another big category is predictions. Because we look at the sky and we see the clouds coming and

28
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:27,300
gathering, we can predict that it's going to rain, for example.

29
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:33,540
So these are predictions and we do lots of those predictions on a daily basis.

30
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:35,190
Arrangements.

31
00:02:35,850 --> 00:02:47,220
If we need to travel somewhere, we buy bus tickets or plane tickets or we buy tickets to the theatre.

32
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:54,990
All of those things are quite certain in the future, because we already have the ticket

33
00:02:54,990 --> 00:03:04,170
and the ticket says that the train leaves, I don't know, 10:30 So it's not even a plan at this time,

34
00:03:04,170 --> 00:03:05,610
but it's an arrangement.

35
00:03:05,610 --> 00:03:13,770
Everything is arranged, everything is 100% definite, like 99% definite that it's going to be like

36
00:03:13,770 --> 00:03:14,100
that.

37
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:21,240
And it's again, it's future tense, but it's our arrangements and we talk about them a lot. Decisions.

38
00:03:21,870 --> 00:03:32,760
And here I mean instant decisions about something that I want to do in the future. Then promises. We are

39
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:33,240
people,

40
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:35,910
yeah, we promise things to each other.

41
00:03:35,910 --> 00:03:36,180
Yeah.

42
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:44,910
And again promises are usually about the future and we need to formulate our promises somehow.

43
00:03:45,420 --> 00:03:50,580
Offers, offers and suggestions as well, and future facts.

44
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:54,870
Maybe we don't know everything about the future.

45
00:03:56,280 --> 00:04:03,810
Maybe we cannot predict everything or we are not sure 100% about what's going to be in the future,

46
00:04:03,810 --> 00:04:06,270
what the future is going to bring.

47
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:16,529
But sometimes we speak about future facts, something that is looks like it's going to be this way and

48
00:04:16,529 --> 00:04:17,420
not the other way.

49
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:17,570
Yes.

50
00:04:17,589 --> 00:04:22,320
So it's pretty certain and we take it as a future fact.

51
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:25,170
So, again, we speak sometimes about that.

52
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:36,270
So these are all of the categories, maybe not all of them, but the major categories that that define

53
00:04:36,690 --> 00:04:42,270
what tense and what grammar structure we need to use in order to talk about all of those things.

54
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:50,520
And now what I would like you to look at sentences, example sentences.

55
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:53,160
There are lots of sentences.

56
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:59,640
I will read all of them and you can see on your screen that

57
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:04,310
the grammar structure is underlined in each sentence.

58
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:07,760
Don't worry if you don't understand all of them.

59
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:15,440
This is exactly why I want to give this list of examples first, and then we are going to go on and

60
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:24,260
look at different grammar structures and you will revise or you will learn them if you don't know them

61
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:24,680
yet.

62
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:27,650
So let's go to the sentences.

63
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:30,350
I'm going to buy a new laptop.

64
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:34,820
So the grammar structure here is I am going to buy.

65
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:38,700
The first release isn't going to be good.

66
00:05:38,710 --> 00:05:40,690
There are too many issues with it.

67
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:46,920
We might need to switch to virtual meetings due to pandemic.

68
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:50,200
I'm working till late today.

69
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:51,900
I have a deadline to meet.

70
00:05:52,950 --> 00:05:54,370
We're going for drinks today.

71
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:55,530
Would you like to join?

72
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,770
And some more here.

73
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:02,890
I have problems with my Internet banking app.

74
00:06:02,910 --> 00:06:04,230
I'll call the support.

75
00:06:05,650 --> 00:06:06,610
Just between us.

76
00:06:06,610 --> 00:06:08,470
I'm going to quit in August.

77
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:10,660
Sure, I won't tell anybody.

78
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:12,130
That's your news to share.

79
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:14,860
Go on for lunch

80
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:15,280
break.

81
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:17,080
I'll handle this request for you.

82
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:20,170
You'll love this new movie.

83
00:06:20,170 --> 00:06:22,150
It's exactly to your taste.

84
00:06:23,230 --> 00:06:25,280
Shall I show you around the office?

85
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:26,270
That would be great.

86
00:06:26,290 --> 00:06:26,920
Thanks.

87
00:06:27,970 --> 00:06:29,890
The course starts in October.

88
00:06:31,330 --> 00:06:31,750
All right.

89
00:06:31,750 --> 00:06:36,010
So these are all of the examples that I have prepared for you.

90
00:06:36,550 --> 00:06:44,650
And if you paid attention every time the grammar structure is underlined and there are different grammar

91
00:06:44,650 --> 00:06:45,250
structures.

92
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:45,580
Yes.

93
00:06:45,610 --> 00:06:46,660
Here we can see.

94
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:48,010
I will call.

95
00:06:48,010 --> 00:06:49,510
I won't tell.

96
00:06:50,890 --> 00:06:51,990
Here we can see.

97
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:53,020
Shall I?

98
00:06:53,410 --> 00:06:56,200
So it's a new word here coming up, shall I?

99
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:59,230
Sometimes you can even see present

100
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:00,160
simple.

101
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:02,110
The course starts.

102
00:07:02,110 --> 00:07:03,430
It's actually present

103
00:07:03,430 --> 00:07:03,910
simple.

104
00:07:03,910 --> 00:07:07,630
But we know that all of the sentences are about future.

105
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:09,640
So we will talk about that.

106
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:19,660
And in the first part, we saw structures like I'm going to we saw might, might need, we saw the present

107
00:07:19,660 --> 00:07:24,820
continuous structure, I'm working to late today, and things like that.

108
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:33,250
So now let's dive in and let's see which grammar structures we need to use for which situations in the

109
00:07:33,250 --> 00:07:33,760
future.

110
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:40,450
And again, keep in mind that we have those categories and now we are going to look at structures and

111
00:07:40,450 --> 00:07:46,570
try to connect them with these categories for future.

112
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:56,280
So the first grammar structure I want to talk about is be going to plus infinitive.

113
00:07:57,170 --> 00:08:02,320
So where do we use this grammar structure?

114
00:08:02,330 --> 00:08:04,010
What do we use it for?

115
00:08:04,100 --> 00:08:08,960
And here we have the rule - used for future plans and intentions.

116
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:12,710
So remember this slide again?

117
00:08:13,670 --> 00:08:16,430
Plans and intentions.

118
00:08:16,430 --> 00:08:16,640
Yeah.

119
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:21,560
I probably should have written plans and intentions here, but you get my point.

120
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:25,580
So used for future plans and intentions.

121
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:34,280
Every time you are talking about your plan, it is very good and very safe to use this grammar structure.

122
00:08:34,309 --> 00:08:36,950
Be going to plus infinitive.

123
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:45,200
So the example sentence again here. He is going to raise money for his startup through crowdfunding.

124
00:08:45,590 --> 00:08:50,390
He is going to raise money because this is his plan.

125
00:08:50,390 --> 00:08:52,580
This is what he plans to do.

126
00:08:54,080 --> 00:08:57,290
I'm going to write a motivational book on leadership.

127
00:08:57,710 --> 00:08:59,480
Again, it's my plan.

128
00:09:01,280 --> 00:09:08,780
That's why I don't say I will write, but I express my plan.

129
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:10,040
It's my intention.

130
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:11,960
That's why I'm using

131
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:13,430
I'm going to write.

132
00:09:15,770 --> 00:09:19,070
We're not going to go to the conference. Again,

133
00:09:19,070 --> 00:09:20,150
it's our plan.

134
00:09:20,270 --> 00:09:21,680
Negative form.

135
00:09:24,290 --> 00:09:24,950
All right.

136
00:09:24,950 --> 00:09:33,470
The next rule here where we can use be going to plus infinitive is for predictions.

137
00:09:33,770 --> 00:09:37,150
So plans, intentions and predictions as well.

138
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:42,380
But here we need to pay attention to one condition.

139
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:48,500
This prediction has to be based on something.

140
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:53,720
So it's not just the prediction out of out of the blue.

141
00:09:54,110 --> 00:09:57,340
So it's not something that I just made up.

142
00:09:58,040 --> 00:10:05,450
It's the prediction that is based supported by some evidence or something around me.

143
00:10:05,930 --> 00:10:08,480
For example, look at those clouds.

144
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:09,770
It's going to rain.

145
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:14,810
It's not because I thought that maybe it's going to rain.

146
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:17,350
It's not just a wild prediction.

147
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:20,930
It's the prediction that is based on some evidence.

148
00:10:21,080 --> 00:10:22,030
Look at the sky.

149
00:10:22,040 --> 00:10:23,330
I see the clouds.

150
00:10:24,280 --> 00:10:28,000
This is the result of this logical connection.

151
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:28,220
Yes.

152
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:34,690
So it's based on some logic or Our service is going to be popular.

153
00:10:34,690 --> 00:10:38,260
There are no alternatives on the market yet. Again,

154
00:10:39,310 --> 00:10:45,110
I base this sentence on the logic that there are no alternatives.

155
00:10:45,130 --> 00:10:52,330
That's why I make the the conclusion that the service is going to be popular.

156
00:10:52,330 --> 00:10:56,800
So it's a prediction which is based on some kind of logic here.

157
00:10:58,150 --> 00:10:59,310
This is important.

158
00:10:59,320 --> 00:10:59,680
Good.

159
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:06,220
So we use be going to for plans, intentions and predictions based on some evidence.

160
00:11:07,810 --> 00:11:10,270
Good, done with going to.

161
00:11:11,260 --> 00:11:13,300
Let's look at the next one.

162
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:20,340
The second point here is might, could, may plus infinitive.

163
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:22,300
Now what is this here?

164
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:31,030
When we plan something we use be going to but not in all of the cases

165
00:11:31,030 --> 00:11:33,580
our plan is 100%.

166
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:42,820
Sometimes we think or we plan to do something, but we are not 100% sure that it's going to be like

167
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:43,300
that.

168
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:45,280
We have a doubt there.

169
00:11:45,430 --> 00:11:55,300
So when we are less sure in our plans, our intentions or the predictions, then we use might plus

170
00:11:55,300 --> 00:11:58,390
infinitive or could or may plus infinitive.

171
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:00,670
Let's look at the examples.

172
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:05,200
I might book a day off next Friday and have a long weekend.

173
00:12:06,230 --> 00:12:07,310
Not sure yet.

174
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:10,210
I'm considering it.

175
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:13,000
Maybe it's my plan.

176
00:12:14,030 --> 00:12:16,310
I'm not really sure if I'm going to do it.

177
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:17,780
So I'm considering.

178
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:19,490
That's why it's not definite.

179
00:12:19,490 --> 00:12:20,930
It's not 100%.

180
00:12:20,930 --> 00:12:22,430
I'm less sure about it.

181
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,790
And I make it visible in this sentence.

182
00:12:25,790 --> 00:12:27,260
I say I might book.

183
00:12:28,790 --> 00:12:32,060
He may be surprised to see me at the meeting tomorrow.

184
00:12:32,420 --> 00:12:34,730
We say he may be surprised. Again,

185
00:12:34,730 --> 00:12:35,500
I don't know.

186
00:12:35,510 --> 00:12:37,820
Maybe he's not going to be surprised.

187
00:12:38,060 --> 00:12:47,480
But I give this like a possibility that this may happen or this could happen.

188
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:52,540
And the last one, we could go to a Mexican place for lunch.

189
00:12:52,550 --> 00:12:53,540
What do you say?

190
00:12:53,720 --> 00:12:59,540
So all of the words work the same in this situation.

191
00:12:59,540 --> 00:13:05,300
Might, could or may you can use either of them with the same meaning.

192
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:11,750
We plan something or we predict something, but we are less sure that it's going to be like that.

